// Resolving a pointer to the block that was written from that address.
//
// **The scope is one ID's own data, not the file.** An address in a .blend is
// only meaningful inside the run of DATA blocks that immediately follows the ID
// block it belongs to, because Blender builds a fresh map per ID while reading
// and throws it away afterwards (`read_data_into_datamap` in readfile.c, then
// `oldnewmap_clear`).
//
// That is not a detail -- it is the whole correctness of this module. Blender
// allocates a temporary array per mesh while writing, writes it, and frees it, so
// the allocator hands back **the same address every time**: all 29 meshes of the
// showroom record `dna_attributes` as 0x5ff8352cd535dcb0, and 29 different blocks
// each claim that address inside their own run. A file-global map therefore gives
// every mesh the first mesh's geometry -- which is exactly what it did, and why
// 45% of triangles indexed past the end of the mesh they belonged to.
//
// The check that hid it was weak: comparing a resolved `position` array's length
// against the mesh's own `totvert` passes when the array belongs to a different
// mesh of the same size, and this file has many. The check that found it compares
// against Blender's own reading of every mesh -- 29/29 now agree on vertex,
// polygon and loop counts and on the first vertex's coordinates.
//
// bl_st[13] and bl_st[14] hold the current run; bl_scope sets them.

bl_at(word wp) {
  int k = 0 - 1;
  int i = (import bl_st)[13];
  while (i < (import bl_st)[14] && k < 0) {
    k = bl_apick(i, wp);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int k;

bl_apick(int i, word wp) {
  int k = 0 - 1;
  if ((import bl_ba)[i] = wp) { k = i; }
} return int k;

// The low half of a 32-bit field, read as an unsigned quantity: bl_le32 builds it
// with a multiply that makes the top bit negative in an int, and an address's low
// half is unsigned.
bl_ule32(int p) {
  word wp = asset_at(p + 3);
} return word wp * 16777216 + asset_at(p) + asset_at(p + 1) * 256 + asset_at(p + 2) * 65536;
